
{"id":"cd408c83-6283-4eb7-b7c8-573e06cfdf71","object":"chat.completion","created":1784987044,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that performs ddmin (delta debugging) with unresolved items. The task is SE-DD-01. The output must be a JSON object with keys: minimal_failure (array of strings), outcome (string), tests (integer). The structural schema is provided.\n\nWe have to parse the case payload. It contains: items (list of strings), required_failure_items (list of strings), unresolved_without (list of strings), and possibly _case_nonce (ignore). The goal is to find a minimal subset of items that still causes failure (FAIL outcome). But there is also concept of UNRESOLVED: some subsets may be UNRESOLVED, which do not permit reduction. The procedure: ddmin with unresolved semantics. The registered properties: one_minimal_result, unresolved_semantics, registered_call_order, termination.\n\nInterpretation: We need to apply delta debugging algorithm that considers subsets of items. The oracle is deterministic: given a set of items (subset of items), we determine outcome: FAIL if the set includes all required_failure_items? Or maybe there is a specific oracle? The candidate artifact description is vague. But the fixture payloads show: \"items\" list, \"required_failure_items\" list, \"unresolved_without\" list. Probably the oracle is: A set causes FAIL if it contains all required_failure_items? And UNRESOLVED if it does not contain any of the unresolved_without items? Actually the description: \"unresolved_semantics\" – items that are required for the test to be resolved? Or maybe the oracle is defined as: outcome = FAIL if the set includes all required_failure_items; else if the set does not include any item from unresolved_without, then outcome = UNRESOLVED; else outcome = PASS? But need to be careful.\n\nLook at fixture cases. Case 0: items d0..d7, required_failure_items = [\"d1\",\"d4\"], unresolved_without = []. For any subset, if it contains both d1 and d4, it's FAIL; if not, it's PASS (since no unresolved). Case 1: required_failure_items = [\"d0\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without = [] – similar. Case 2: required_failure_items = [\"d4\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without = [\"d0\"]. So if the subset does not include d0, then it's UNRESOLVED (since unresolved_without means \"if without these items, it's unresolved\"?). The phrase \"unresolved_without\" suggests that if the subset does NOT contain any of these items, the outcome is UNRESOLVED. So if subset contains d0, then it's resolved; and then if it contains required_failure_items, it's FAIL else PASS. But also if subset does not contain d0, outcome is UNRESOLVED. That makes sense.\n\nThus oracle function: given set S (which is a subset of items), check if any of unresolved_without is missing from S? Actually \"unresolved_without\" means if the set is without these items, it's unresolved. So if S does not contain at least one of unresolved_without? Or all? The list is \"unresolved_without\": items whose absence makes the test unresolved. Typically delta debugging with unresolved has a condition: if the test case is missing a necessary component, it's unresolved. Here they list the items that are necessary? The name ambiguous. But typical ddmin with unresolved: some subsets are UNRESOLVED if they are missing essential items required for the test to run. So unresolved_without list are items that must be present for the outcome to be resolvable. So if the subset does not contain all of unresolved_without, it's UNRESOLVED. If it contains all required_failure_items, it's FAIL else PASS.\n\nCheck case2: unresolved_without = [\"d0\"]. So if subset lacks d0, it's UNRESOLVED. If it contains d0, then check if it contains both d4 and d7 => FAIL else PASS. That seems plausible.\n\nSo we need to implement ddmin algorithm with this oracle. The algorithm: start with full set of items (should be FAIL). Partition into subsets, test each subset and its complement. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size. Return the 1-minimal failure (i.e., minimal set that still fails). Test count is number of oracle calls.\n\nWe must output minimal_failure list of strings, outcome string (should be \"FAIL\" presumably, since we are minimizing failure-inducing set), and tests integer.\n\nImplementation: define a function `outcome(subset)` that returns \"PASS\", \"FAIL\", or \"UNRESOLVED\" based on the payload fields.\n\nThen ddmin: start with full set = set(items). Verify that outcome(full) == \"FAIL\". Then call ddmin recursively.\n\nPseudocode for ddmin (from Zeller's delta debugging):\n\ndef ddmin(case_set, n=2):\n    if len(case_set) == 1:\n        return case_set, tests_used\n    # partition into n subsets\n    subsets = partition(case_set, n)\n    for subset in subsets:\n        if outcome(subset) == \"FAIL\":\n            return ddmin(subset, 2)  # reduce granularity\n    # if no subset fails, test complements\n    complements = [case_set - subset for subset in subsets]\n    for complement in complements:\n        if outcome(complement) == \"FAIL\":\n            return ddmin(complement, max(n-1,2))\n    # if no complement fails, increase granularity\n    if n < len(case_set):\n        return ddmin(case_set, min(2*n, len(case_set)))\n    else:\n        return case_set, tests_used\n\nBut need to adapt for unresolved: if outcome is UNRESOLVED, we treat it like PASS: not reducing. Also note: The complement tests: we test complements only if none of the subsets failed. And if a complement fails, we reduce to that complement. Then granularity decreases (n = max(n-1,2)). If no complement fails, we increase granularity (n = min(2*n, len(case_set))).\n\nWe need to count tests: increment each time we call outcome.\n\nAlso we need to handle the list ordering? The \"registered_call_order\" suggests that subsets and complements should be tested in order of their source position in items. So we must ensure the partition respects order? Items are given in a specific order. The items list is ordered. ddmin typically operates on ordered sets (e.g., list of indices). So we should treat items as an ordered list, and subsets as contiguous intervals? Actually classic ddmin for minimizing failure-inducing input (like a list of changes) uses subsets that are contiguous intervals of the list. But here items are just identifiers, not necessarily ordered? However the problem says \"ordered configuration\" and \"exact subset/complement schedule\" and \"ranking and traversal ties use the source position or identifier\". Since items are given as a list, we can treat the order as the given list order. So we should partition the list into contiguous chunks.\n\nSo we'll work with indices: a list of indices from 0 to len(items)-1 representing the order. Then subsets are intervals of these indices. The complement is then the set of indices not in that interval (but preserving order? For complement we just need the set of indices, but the order matters for partitioning? The algorithm usually tests subsets and their complements as sets. Since we are using intervals, the complement is two intervals (left and right) which is not contiguous. But ddmin algorithm typically works on any subset as a set, not necessarily contiguous. However the classic ddmin on interval partitions? Actually the algorithm partitions the set into n subsets, but the subsets themselves are not necessarily contiguous in the original ordering; they are arbitrary subsets that form a partition of the set. To be deterministic, we need a fixed partitioning method. The problem mentions \"exact subset/complement schedule\" and \"registered_call_order\". Probably we should follow Zeller's ddmin: split the set into n subsets, but not necessarily contiguous. However to avoid ambiguity, we need a deterministic partition based on position. The common approach: split into n roughly equal contiguous parts. That is typical in delta debugging. We'll adopt that.\n\nBut careful: The algorithm expects that subsets are tested in order, and complements are tested in order after all subsets. We'll produce subsets as list of lists (each list being the items in that subset in original order). The complements are the rest of items (set difference). But when we reduce to a complement, we need to preserve the original ordering for further partitioning. So we need to keep track of the ordered list of items (the current configuration). So we'll represent state as an ordered list of item strings (the current set we are trying to minimize). When we create subsets, we split that list into n contiguous segments. For each subset, we compute outcome on that list (converted to set? Actually we need to check if that subset alone causes FAIL? But the oracle expects a set of items. So we can pass the subset as a list (order not needed for oracle). Similarly for complement, it's the list of items from the current set excluding those in the subset, but preserving order (i.e., concatenation of remaining segments). The outcome function takes a list of strings (or set) and returns string.\n\nWe must also handle the case where outcome for a subset or complement is UNRESOLVED. According to spec: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So if outcome(subset) is UNRESOLVED, we skip that subset (do not reduce). If outcome(complement) is UNRESOLVED, we also skip. That matches treatment as PASS.\n\nNow, we need to implement the recursion with counting tests. We'll have a counter (nonlocal variable or class). Return minimal_failure list, outcome (should be \"FAIL\"), tests count.\n\nWe must also validate that the initial configuration yields FAIL. If not, maybe raise ValueError? The spec says \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" So we should check that outcome(full_items) == \"FAIL\". If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso need to handle edge cases: What if there are no items? Probably not.\n\nNow implement solve(case):\n\n- Extract items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without from case payload (case is dict). Also ignore _case_nonce.\n- Verify that items is a list, required_failure_items is list, unresolved_without is list. Also validate that all required_failure_items are in items (otherwise set cannot contain them). If not, raise ValueError.\n- Define oracle function:\n\ndef outcome(config):\n    s = set(config)\n    # If config does not contain all items from unresolved_without, return \"UNRESOLVED\".\n    if not all(item in s for item in unresolved_without):\n        return \"UNRESOLVED\"\n    # Else, if it contains all required_failure_items, return \"FAIL\"\n    if all(item in s for item in required_failure_items):\n        return \"FAIL\"\n    # Else PASS\n    return \"PASS\"\n\n- Then ddmin:\n\ndef ddmin(current_list, n=2):\n    if len(current_list) == 1:\n        return current_list, 0  # no test needed? Actually we need to test that single element. But length 1, we already know it fails? Actually we need to verify. The recursion ensures we only reduce when subset fails, so when we get to size 1, we already know it fails (since we came from a failing subset). But still we should count the test for that single set? In classic ddmin, when you reduce to a single element, you would have tested that element as part of the last reduction. So no extra test needed. But to be safe, we can count tests at each call to outcome. We'll count all outcome calls.\n\nSo we'll have a test_count variable incrementing each time we call outcome.\n\n- Partition: compute k = min(n, len(current_list)). Spit into contiguous segments of roughly equal size. Use a loop: size = (len(current_list) + i) // k? We'll compute sizes.\n\n- For each segment (subset_list), call outcome(subset_list). If outcome is \"FAIL\", then recursively ddmin on that subset_list with n=2. If outcome is \"PASS\" or \"UNRESOLVED\", continue.\n\nIf no subset fails, then compute complements: for each segment, complement_list = current_list without that segment (preserve order). Test each complement in order. If outcome(complement_list) == \"FAIL\", then recursively ddmin on complement_list with n = max(n-1, 2). If none, then if n < len(current_list): recursively ddmin on same current_list with n = min(2*n, len(current_list)). If n already >= len(current_list), then we cannot increase further; return current_list (minimal failure) and test count.\n\nBut careful: In the recursion, we need to return minimal_failure list and also the total tests used. So we need to accumulate tests across recursive calls.\n\nImplementation plan: Use a nonlocal variable or pass a mutable counter. Since we are defining solve as a function, we can define helper functions inside solve that use closure to access case payload and a counter variable.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = case[\"required_failure_items\"]\n    unresolved = case[\"unresolved_without\"]\n\n    # validation\n    if not isinstance(items, list) or not isinstance(required, list) or not isinstance(unresolved, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid payload structure\")\n    if not all(item in items for item in required):\n        raise ValueError(\"required_failure_items not all in items\")\n    # optional: check unresolved items are in items? Probably not required but could be.\n\n    test_count = 0\n\n    def outcome(config):\n        nonlocal test_count\n        test_count += 1\n        s = set(config)\n        if not all(x in s for x in unresolved):\n            return \"UNRESOLVED\"\n        if all(x in s for x in required):\n            return \"FAIL\"\n        return \"PASS\"\n\n    full_config = list(items)  # ordered\n    if outcome(full_config) != \"FAIL\":\n        raise ValueError(\"Initial configuration must be FAIL\")\n\n    def partition(lst, n):\n        # returns list of sublists (contiguous)\n        k = min(n, len(lst))\n        # compute base size and remainder\n        size = len(lst) // k\n        remainder = len(lst) % k\n        sublists = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(k):\n            extra = 1 if i < remainder else 0\n            end = start + size + extra\n            sublists.append(lst[start:end])\n            start = end\n        return sublists\n\n    def ddmin(current, n=2):\n        nonlocal test_count\n        if len(current) == 1:\n            return list(current)  # minimal failure (already known to fail)\n        # Subset tests\n        subsets = partition(current, n)\n        for subset in subsets:\n            res = outcome(subset)\n            if res == \"FAIL\":\n                # reduce to this subset, reset n to 2\n                return ddmin(subset, 2)\n        # Complement tests\n        # Build complements\n        complements = []\n        start = 0\n        for subset in subsets:\n            complement = current[:start] + current[start+len(subset):]\n            complements.append(complement)\n            start += len(subset)\n        for complement in complements:\n            res = outcome(complement)\n            if res == \"FAIL\":\n                # reduce to complement, n = max(n-1,2)\n                new_n = max(n-1, 2)\n                return ddmin(complement, new_n)\n        # If no reduction, increase granularity\n        if n < len(current):\n            new_n = min(2*n, len(current))\n            return ddmin(current, new_n)\n        else:\n            # cannot increase further, current is minimal\n            return list(current)\n\n    minimal = ddmin(full_config)\n    # final outcome should be FAIL, but we can re-verify\n    final_outcome = outcome(minimal)  # should be FAIL\n    # but we already counted this test; ensure consistent\n    # minimal_failure should be list of strings\n    return {\n        \"minimal_failure\": minimal,\n        \"outcome\": \"FAIL\",  # final outcome is always FAIL per algorithm\n        \"tests\": test_count\n    }\n\nBut note: The ddmin function currently doesn't return test count. We need to return both minimal set and test count. So modify ddmin to return a tuple (minimal_list, tests_used_in_subtree). But since test_count is nonlocal and incremented globally, we can just accumulate. However the recursion will keep using the same nonlocal test_count, which is fine. But we need to ensure that when we return, test_count includes all tests performed. So we can simply return minimal list and then at the end, test_count is total.\n\nBut careful: In ddmin, when we do \"return ddmin(subset, 2)\" we are recursing and the test counts will be added. That's fine. So final test_count will be correct.\n\nWe also need to handle the case where after all tests, we can't reduce further and return current. That's minimal.\n\nNow test with fixture cases.\n\nCase 0: items = d0..d7, required = [d1,d4], unresolved=[].\nFull set outcome: contains d1,d4 -> FAIL.\nPartition n=2: subsets: [d0,d1,d2,d3] and [d4,d5,d6,d7]. Test subset1: does it contain d1 and d4? d1 yes, d4 no -> PASS. Subset2: d4 yes, d1 no -> PASS. Complements: complement1 (d4,d5,d6,d7) -> same as subset2 -> PASS. complement2 (d0,d1,d2,d3) -> PASS. No reduction. n=2 < len=8, increase n to 4. Partition into 4 subsets of size 2: [d0,d1], [d2,d3], [d4,d5], [d6,d7]. Test subsets: [d0,d1] contains d1 but not d4 -> PASS. [d2,d3]: PASS. [d4,d5]: contains d4 but not d1 -> PASS. [d6,d7]: PASS. Complements: complement of [d0,d1] = [d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] contains d4 and d1? Wait, it contains d4 and d1? No, d1 is removed. So it has d4 but no d1 -> PASS. Similarly others. So no reduction. n=4 <8, increase to 8. Partition into 8 singles. Test each single: only d1 and d4 would be FAIL? Actually single d1: contains d1 but not d4 -> PASS. Single d4: contains d4 but not d1 -> PASS. All PASS. Complements: complement of [d0] is rest: [d1..d7] which contains d1 and d4 -> FAIL! So complement of first single (d0) fails. So we reduce to complement = [d1,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] with n = max(8-1=7,2)=7? Actually n was 8, max(n-1,2)=7. Then we recursively ddmin on this set of size 7 with n=7? That could be many steps. The algorithm will eventually isolate d1 and d4 as minimal failure. So minimal_failure should be [\"d1\",\"d4\"]? But is it 1-minimal? For this case, the minimal failure set could be any set containing both d1 and d4. The algorithm should return one minimal set. It might return [\"d1\",\"d4\"] if both are needed? Actually if we remove either, it PASSes. So minimal failure set is exactly {d1,d4}. But the algorithm might return a set like [d1,d2,d4] if it reduces to a complement that includes extra items? But the algorithm reduces only when a subset or complement fails. Let's simulate: after complement of [d0] fails, we recurse on [d1..d7] with n=7. Then partition into 7 subsets? That would be 7 singles? Actually n=7, len=7, so partition into 7 subsets each of size 1: [d1],[d2],...,[d7]. Test subsets: each single fails? No, only [d1] and [d4] would be PASS (since missing other), all PASS. Then complements: complement of [d1] is [d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] contains d4 but not d1 -> PASS. complement of [d2] is [d1,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] contains both d1 and d4 -> FAIL! So reduce to complement of [d2] = [d1,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] with n = max(6,2)=6. Continue. Eventually we will remove d2,d3,d5,d6,d7 one by one through complements, finally resulting in [d1,d4]. That is the minimal failure. So output should be [\"d1\",\"d4\"], outcome \"FAIL\", tests count many.\n\nWe need to ensure that the order of testing subsets and complements follows \"registered_call_order\" – i.e., the order of elements in items list. Our partition and complement order follows the original order, so that should be fine.\n\nNow test case 2: unresolved_without = [\"d0\"]. Full set contains d0 -> resolved, contains d4,d7 -> FAIL. Partition n=2: subsets: [d0,d1,d2,d3] and [d4,d5,d6,d7]. Test subset1: contains d0 but not both d4,d7 -> PASS. Subset2: does not contain d0 -> UNRESOLVED. Since only FAIL reduces, we do not reduce. Complements: complement1 = [d4,d5,d6,d7] -> does not contain d0 -> UNRESOLVED. complement2 = [d0,d1,d2,d3] -> resolved, missing d4,d7 -> PASS. No reduction. Increase n. Eventually we might find a complement that fails while also containing d0. For example, if we partition into singles: subsets all singles: d0 is UNRESOLVED? Actually single d0 contains d0 -> resolved, missing d4,d7 -> PASS. d4 single: does not contain d0 -> UNRESOLVED. etc. Complements: complement of d0 is all others [d1,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] -> does not contain d0 -> UNRESOLVED. So no reduction at n=8. Then increase n? Already at len=8, so cannot increase. Then current set [d0..d7] is minimal? But is it? Actually we want minimal failure that still fails. Since missing d0 causes UNRESOLVED, any minimal failing set must include d0. And must include both d4 and d7. Could we reduce to [d0,d4,d7]? That would be resolved (has d0) and contains both -> FAIL. But can algorithm find it? Let's see: after no reduction at n=8, we return full set. But is there a path? The algorithm tests subsets and complements. At n=8, it tests each single, all PASS or UNRESOLVED. Complements: complement of any single that is not d0 will be UNRESOLVED (if the single removed is not d0, then complement still has d0? Actually complement of d1 is [d0,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] -> contains d0, contains d4,d7 -> FAIL? Wait complement of d1 is all except d1. That set contains d0, d4, d7. That would be FAIL! So complement of d1 fails. Then we reduce to complement of d1 which is [d0,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7]. That set is size 7, contains d0, d4, d7 -> FAIL. Then we recurse with n=7 (max(8-1,2)=7). Then we can continue removing non-essential items. So indeed we can reduce. So minimal_failure should be [d0,d4,d7] (or maybe [d0,d4,d7] in some order). Since order of items is preserved, the minimal set will be [d0,d4,d7]? Actually when we keep reducing by complements of non-essential items, we will remove d2,d3,d5,d6 eventually. The order might end up as [d0,d4,d7] (since d1 may also be removed? d1 is not essential. So yes, the algorithm should produce that. So outcome FAIL.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the algorithm returns the minimal failure as a list of strings preserving the original order? The minimal_failure should be a list of strings, probably in the order they appear in the current configuration after reductions. The original order is the order in items. When we partition and take complements, the order is preserved (since we slice and concatenate). So final list will be a subsequence of items in original order. That should be acceptable. The spec says \"minimal_failure\" is an array of strings. No requirements on ordering beyond being a subset.\n\nNow we need to implement exactly as per algorithm.\n\nEdge Cases: The initial configuration may be FAIL but has size 0? Probably not. Also if required_failure_items is empty? Then any set will be FAIL? But then minimal failure is empty set? However delta debugging expects initial failure. If empty set is FAIL, then minimal failure would be empty set. But the problem likely expects non-empty. We'll handle: if required_failure_items empty, then outcome of empty set is FAIL (since all required items present vacuously). Then algorithm: start with full set, but we want minimal failure: empty set. However ddmin algorithm as defined will not reduce because subsets and complements may also be FAIL (empty set is not in the partition initially). Actually if empty set is FAIL, the initial full set is FAIL. The algorithm will test subsets; if any subset is FAIL, it reduces. But the empty set can only be reached if we partition to include the empty set, which can only happen if we have n>len? Typically not. So we need to handle the case where the minimal failure could be empty set. But spec says \"minimal_failure\" is array of strings, so empty array is possible. We should allow it. In that case, we need to adjust algorithm to consider that the minimal failure could be empty. Standard ddmin can find empty set if the complement of a single element fails (meaning the set without that element still fails, eventually removing all). So algorithm will eventually reduce to empty set. For example, if required_failure_items empty, then any set fails. The algorithm will reduce by removing items one by one. Starting with full set, partition n=2, test subsets: first subset (first half) will fail (since no requirement), so reduce to that subset. Continue until size 1, then partition into 2? Actually when size 1, we return size 1 as minimal, but empty set might be minimal if removing that single item still fails. The algorithm does not test the empty set explicitly. But the complement of the single element (i.e., empty set) could be tested. For size 1, partition n=2? len=1, so n=1 (min(2,1)=1) partition yields one subset of size 1. Subset test: that single element fails (since empty set not tested). Then complement: empty set. If outcome(empty) == FAIL, then we would reduce to empty set with n = max(2-1=1,2)=2? Actually we need to handle the recursion when complement fails and len(current)==1? Let's see: In ddmin, when len(current)==1, we return current (size 1). But if we tested complement and it fails, we should reduce to that complement (size 0). So we need to modify base case: if len(current) == 0, return current. And also allow len(current) > 0 but after complement test, if complement is empty and fails, we recurse with empty set. So we should handle len(current) == 0 as a base case. So modify:\n\nif len(current) == 0:\n    return list(current)  # empty list\n\nAlso when len(current) == 1, we still test subset (single) and complement (empty). If complement fails, we go to empty.\n\nSo we'll adjust.\n\nNow also need to ensure that initial configuration is FAIL; if required_fai